    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_nmm_131'), 'Three modules of the CodeDev API:

@Module({ providers: [UserService, UserRepository], exports: [UserService] })
export class UserModule {}

@Module({
  imports: [UserModule],
  providers: [CourseService, CourseRepository],
  exports: [CourseService],
})
export class CourseModule {}

@Module({ imports: [CourseModule], providers: [EnrollmentService, EnrollmentRepository] })
export class EnrollmentModule {}

@Injectable()
export class EnrollmentService {
  constructor(
    private readonly courses: CourseService,
    private readonly users: UserService,
  ) {}
}

Statement: "The application starts, because EnrollmentModule imports CourseModule, CourseModule imports UserModule, and so UserService is available to EnrollmentService."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. Imports are not transitive: a module sees only the exports of the modules it imports itself. CourseModule exports CourseService but not UserModule, so EnrollmentModule gets CourseService and nothing else. Startup fails with: Nest can''t resolve dependencies of the EnrollmentService (CourseService, ?). Please make sure that the argument UserService at index [1] is available in the EnrollmentModule context. The fix is to add UserModule to EnrollmentModule''s imports, which also documents the dependency. Re-exporting UserModule from CourseModule would work too, but then every importer of CourseModule silently depends on users. The misconception is that the module graph shares everything along a path, the way JavaScript imports load everything they touch.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_nmm_131') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
