WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_nmm_231'), 'The new filter and how it is registered:

// common/domain-error.filter.ts
@Catch(DomainError)
export class DomainErrorFilter implements ExceptionFilter {
  catch(error: DomainError, host: ArgumentsHost) {
    // maps the error and writes an application/problem+json response
  }
}

// main.ts
app.useGlobalFilters(new DomainErrorFilter());

Server log:
TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading ''plan'')
    at EnrollmentsService.enroll (enrollments.service.ts:57:31)

Response to the client:
HTTP/1.1 500 Internal Server Error
Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8

{"statusCode":500,"message":"Internal server error"}

Statement: "This response was written by Nest''s built-in exception filter, not by DomainErrorFilter, because @Catch(DomainError) only receives DomainError instances."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. @Catch(DomainError) tells Nest to call this filter only for exceptions that are instances of DomainError (or its subclasses). A TypeError is not, so Nest falls back to its built-in global exception filter, which answers every exception that is not an HttpException with exactly this body: {"statusCode":500,"message":"Internal server error"}. The Content-Type confirms it: the custom filter would have written application/problem+json. To give unknown errors the same problem format and a correlation id, use @Catch() with no argument and handle the unknown case yourself, still without putting the error''s message in the body. The misconception is that a global filter sees every error; it sees the types listed in @Catch.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_nmm_231') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
