WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_nmm_231'), 'The mobile app''s API client:

// mobile/api/client.ts
// retry a request when it fails with a network error or status >= 500:
// up to 3 retries, after 1 s, 2 s and 4 s. Never retry a 4xx.

Access log before the fix (one tap on Enroll, the learner was already enrolled):
10:41:02.118 POST /enrollments 500 31ms
10:41:03.164 POST /enrollments 500 28ms
10:41:05.201 POST /enrollments 500 30ms
10:41:09.236 POST /enrollments 500 29ms

After the fix, the API answers the same request with:
HTTP/1.1 409 Conflict
Content-Type: application/problem+json

{"type":"https://api.codedev.test/problems/already-enrolled","title":"Already enrolled","status":409, ...}

Statement: "The app will still send this POST four times per tap until its retry policy is changed as well."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. The retries came from the status code, not from the app: a 500 says "the server failed, trying again might work", and the client''s policy retries exactly that. With the domain error mapped to 409, the response falls in the 4xx range the policy never retries, so one tap now produces one request and the app can show "You are already enrolled" from the problem body. Choosing the right status is how the server tells every client, including ones you do not control, whether a retry can help. The misconception is that each client needs its own fix; a correct status code fixes all of them at once.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_nmm_231') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO challenge.question_options (question_id, option_text, is_correct)
SELECT q.id, o.option_text, o.is_correct
FROM q, (VALUES
        ('True', false),
        ('False', true)
    ) AS o(option_text, is_correct);

