API log, one learner double-tapping Enroll in the mobile app (the table has no unique constraint yet):

10:41:07.112 POST /enrollments user=u_1842 course=nestjs-modular-monolith -> 201 enr_9310 (38 ms)
10:41:07.115 POST /enrollments user=u_1842 course=nestjs-modular-monolith -> 201 enr_9311 (36 ms)

Statement: "These two rows can appear even with the duplicate check in the service, so the unique constraint on enrollment (user_id, course_id) is still needed."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. The two requests started 3 ms apart and each took over 30 ms, so both ran findByUserAndCourse before either inserted: both saw no row, both passed the check and both called create. A check-then-insert in application code is a race whenever two requests overlap, and only the database can make the second insert fail, with UNIQUE (user_id, course_id) (the race and the constraint are covered in "Stop duplicate enrollment: races & unique constraints" in SQL & Data Modeling). The service rule is still worth having: it answers the common sequential retry with a clean "existing" result and a good error message, and the repository turns the rare unique violation (SQLSTATE 23505) into the same answer. The misconception is that a rule in the service is enough on its own because each request is handled correctly in isolation.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_nmm_311') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
